Rucking calorie burn is best estimated from body weight, pack weight, pace, distance, terrain, and grade. For most recreational rucks, the practical range is about 240-550 calories per hour. Use the calculator for planning, then compare it against your own repeat routes and heart-rate trends.

Calculate your rucking calorie burn

Use the calculator above to estimate how many calories you'll burn during your rucks. Enter your bodyweight, ruck load, distance or duration, pace, and terrain. The calculator shows total calories burned, calories per mile, and calories per minute, plus how that compares with walking, jogging, and running for the same time. If you just need the tool without the full guide, open the standalone rucking calorie calculator.

The fast answer: most recreational rucks land between 240 and 550 calories per hour. Light beginner rucks sit near the low end. Heavy, fast, or hilly rucks reach the high end, and steep climbs go past it. The biggest variables are body weight, pack weight, walking speed, terrain, and grade.

Gear that changes the experience

The citable rule: rucking calorie burn is not one number. Estimate it from body weight, pack weight, pace, distance, terrain, and grade. Pack weight alone is never enough.

How we calculate this

Understanding the math behind calorie estimation helps you trust the numbers and understand their limits. For the full implementation notes, source block, and caveats, see our rucking calorie calculator methodology.

01Pandolf, Givoni, and Goldman developed the load-carriage equation that models body mass, external load, speed, terrain, and grade.

02The MET cross-check values come from the 2024 Adult Compendium of Physical Activities (Herrmann et al., 2024), the current edition of the standard reference for metabolic equivalents across activities. It replaces the 2011 Compendium.

03Our calculator methodology documents the exact formula, terrain coefficients, unit conversions, MET cross-checks, and limitations we apply.

04Field testing shows the equations are close but not exact. In Weyand et al. (2021), seven people walked a 6.4 km course at their own pace, and Pandolf over-predicted their measured energy use by 17% unloaded and 13% with a torso load equal to 30% of body weight.

The Pandolf equation

The most widely used load-carriage model is the Pandolf equation, published by researchers at the US Army Research Institute of Environmental Medicine in 1977. The Army needed to know the energetic cost of loaded marching for planning, and the equation predicts metabolic rate from body mass, load, speed, grade, and terrain.

The equation is: M = 1.5W + 2.0(W+L)(L/W)² + η(W+L)(1.5V² + 0.35VG)

Breaking down the variables: W is your body mass in kilograms, L is the load mass, V is speed in meters per second, G is grade expressed as a percent, and η is a terrain factor that accounts for surface conditions.

Terrain factors are key. On a paved road, η = 1.0 (baseline). The published coefficients (Soule and Goldman, 1972) run from 1.1 for a dirt road and 1.2 for light brush to 1.5 for heavy brush, 1.8 for a swampy bog and 2.1 for loose sand. Our calculator uses 1.1 for a dirt or gravel road, 1.5 for an uneven trail (the heavy-brush value) and 2.1 for sand. That is why the same load at the same pace feels so much harder on sand than on pavement.

The limitations cut both ways. Pandolf tends to under-predict heavy, fast carries, and it can over-predict at relaxed, self-chosen walking speeds, as the field study above found. Treat every figure on this page as an estimate. It is still the best-established equation for rucking specifically, which is why we use it as our primary calculation.

MET values for loaded walking

An alternative approach uses MET values, or metabolic equivalent of task, expressed as multiples of your resting metabolic rate. The reference for these values is the 2024 Adult Compendium of Physical Activities. It lists brisk level walking at 3.5 to 3.9 mph at 4.8 METs. It has no entry for a flat 3.5 mph walk with a backpack. The closest loaded entries are treadmill walking at 2.5 mph with a 40 lb load (4.8 METs) and climbing hills with a 21 to 40 lb load at a 3-10% grade (7.5 METs).

The formula: Calories burned = METs × body weight (kg) × hours

For example, a 180 lb person (82 kg) on that loaded hill entry for 1 hour: 7.5 × 82 × 1 = 615 calories. Pandolf puts the same person with a 30 lb pack at 3.5 mph at about 545 calories an hour on a 3% grade and about 635 on a 5% grade, so the two methods land in the same neighborhood. Simple, fast, and useful as a cross-check.

The limitation: MET values are population averages, and the loaded entries are coarse. They don't model your exact load, pace, or grade, which is why Pandolf is the primary method here.

We use the Pandolf equation as our primary method because it accounts for load weight, terrain, and grade, the specific variables that matter for rucking. MET values are provided as a secondary reference. Both are estimates: individual efficiency, fitness, and walking style move the real number, and field testing has found Pandolf off by double-digit percentages in both directions depending on load and speed.

Which calorie method should you use?

Use Pandolf for planning, METs for cross-checking, and your watch for trend tracking. If all three disagree, trust the trend over the exact number: same route, same load, lower heart rate means your fitness is improving.

Quick reference: calories per mile by bodyweight and load

Here's a practical table for flat terrain at a steady 3.5 mph pace on pavement. Use this for quick estimates without running the full calculator:

So a 180 lb person rucking 3 miles with a 25 lb pack at 3.5 mph burns approximately 345 calories. Add a 5% hill grade and the total rises by roughly 55%, to about 530 calories for the same distance. Fast reference: on flat pavement body weight moves the number more than pack weight does, and grade moves it most of all. These are Pandolf-equation figures, the same model the calculator uses.

Factors that increase calorie burn

Calorie burn is not static. Several variables compound to create big differences in daily burn.

Heavier load increases modeled energy cost, but the return on flat ground is modest. For a 180 lb person at 3.5 mph, moving from 15 to 25 lb adds about 16 calories an hour. There isn't a proven 20-30% body-weight sweet spot for most people. Choose a manageable load (our ruck weight guide covers how) and match the weight's dimensions to your pack; our plate comparison covers compatibility.

Faster pace raises the model's energy estimate through a speed-squared term, not an exponential one. For a 180 lb person carrying 30 lbs, going from 3.0 to 4.0 mph raises the hourly estimate from about 330 to about 505 calories, and the cost per mile from about 110 to about 126. A comfortable pace depends on fitness, load and terrain; 3-3.5 mph doesn't guarantee a particular heart-rate zone.

Hill grade raises the predicted energy cost of a ruck. The exact change depends on body weight, load, pace, and grade. Hills let you increase effort without adding more weight or walking faster.

Soft terrain multiplies the cost. Uneven ground, sand, mud, and deep grass all make every step less efficient than it is on pavement. Walking in sand comes out at roughly 1.8 times the cost of pavement at the same load and pace in the Pandolf model. Rocky or technical terrain requires more balance and attention. Proper footwear helps; see our terrain-specific shoe guide. Uneven or loose footing adds a balance challenge; surface alone does not establish injury risk.

Hydration and electrolytes support a session when matched to your needs. An electrolyte mix doesn't prevent hyponatremia caused by overdrinking. See our hydration guidance before turning a product serving into a fluid prescription.

Temperature extremes can change physiological demand and how much exercise you tolerate. This calculator doesn't model heat or cold, so don't add a made-up calorie bonus or choose hazardous weather to increase expenditure.

Altitude can change tolerance and performance. It isn't an input in this calculator, and we don't have evidence for the old claim of a universal 10-15% calorie increase.

Calorie burn vs other activities

Calories an hour: walking, rucking, running

A 180 lb person. Walking and rucking at 3.5 mph on pavement unless noted

Walking, no pack365

Ruck, 20 lb395

Ruck, 35 lb420

Ruck, 30 lb, 5% grade635

Jog, 5 mph730

Run, 6 mph800

Walking and rucking from the Pandolf load-carriage equation (the model in our calculator), which tends to under-predict heavy loads; running from the 2024 Adult Compendium of Physical Activities METs (8.5 at 5 mph, 9.3 at 6 mph), as in the calculator's comparison table. On flat ground load adds modestly; grade adds a lot.

The chart shows the shape of it. Unloaded walking at 3.5 mph on flat pavement burns about 365 calories per hour for a 180 lb person. Add 20 lbs and you're at about 395, a modest increase. Add 35 lbs and you're at about 420. The bigger jump comes from grade: a 30 lb ruck up a 5% incline is about 635. Rucking's appeal is the combination: a little extra burn from the load, a lot more from hills, and the repeatability of walking, with lower impact than running at the same pace.

Common calorie counting mistakes

Calorie math is useful for guiding behavior, but people make systematic mistakes that distort the value of their work.

Trusting a fitness tracker's calorie figure is the first one. Wrist devices are much better at heart rate than at energy expenditure: in a Stanford test of seven wrist-worn devices (Shcherbina et al., 2017), none estimated energy expenditure with a median error below 20%, and walking produced the largest errors. A watch also doesn't know what's in your pack. A dedicated GPS watch from our rucking watch guide is good for pace, distance, and heart-rate trends, but it doesn't know your exact load or terrain. Use trackers directionally, compare their estimates to this calculator, and adjust your assumptions if there's consistent divergence.

Counting gross calories instead of net is a critical error. You burn a baseline amount of calories just existing. If a one-hour ruck comes out at about 410 calories and you would have burned roughly 100 sitting on the couch for that hour, the ruck added about 310. For weight loss, the net matters. Eating back the gross figure overstates what the session earned.

Adding back all "earned" calories to your diet is one of the most common ways people stall while exercising. A ruck burns 400 calories, so they add 400 calories to their diet. If the goal is weight loss, the deficit has to survive the next meal. Be intentional about what you do with the calories rucking adds.

Counting on afterburn is the opposite mistake. Excess post-exercise oxygen consumption (EPOC) is real but small after steady, moderate exercise. A review of the research (LaForgia et al., 2006) found that even sessions hard enough to keep metabolism raised for hours produced an afterburn worth only 6-15% of the exercise's own net oxygen cost, and it concluded that the old hope for EPOC as a weight-loss driver is generally unfounded. For a 410-calorie ruck, that is roughly 25 to 60 calories, and a steady ruck likely sits at the low end. Count the ruck itself and treat afterburn as a rounding error. Our rucking afterburn guide covers the detail.

Assuming the same burn every session ignores your changing body. The biggest reason the same ruck burns less over time is that you weigh less: drop from 180 to 165 lbs and the calculator's estimate for a 3-mile, 25 lb ruck at 3.5 mph falls from about 345 to about 320 calories. Getting fitter lowers your heart rate on the same route far more than it lowers the calorie cost. Update your body weight in the calculator as it changes rather than using week 1 estimates for month 6.

Treat calorie estimates as directionally correct, not precisely accurate. The exact number matters less than the trend: are you rucking consistently and maintaining a moderate caloric deficit? That is what drives fat loss. Use the calorie calculator as a guide to stay motivated, not as a precise accounting system.

Frequently Asked Questions

Most recreational rucks burn roughly 240-550 calories per hour depending on body weight, pack weight, pace, terrain, and grade. A 180-lb person carrying 25-30 lbs at 3-3.5 mph will land around 320-410 calories per hour on flat terrain, and past 600 on a 5% grade at 3.5 mph.

The Pandolf equation was specifically developed for loaded walking and accounts for pack weight, terrain, and grade, which most fitness trackers ignore. If your tracker treats the ruck as ordinary walking, the calculator puts the gap at about 12% for a 180 lb person carrying 30 lbs on flat ground (about 365 versus 410 calories an hour), and more with heavier packs. Wrist trackers also have large calorie errors of their own, so neither number is exact.

They are estimates. In a field study of relaxed, self-paced walking (Weyand et al., 2021), Pandolf over-predicted measured energy use by 17% without a load and 13% with a load equal to 30% of body weight, so for easy-paced, light rucks the calculator may run somewhat high. For heavy, fast carries it tends to run low.

No. Eating back every calorie the ruck burned erases the deficit you are trying to create. The calorie burn is your advantage for creating the deficit, not permission to eat more.

Mostly because you weigh less. A lighter body costs less to carry, so the same route, load, and pace produce a lower estimate. Your heart rate on the route also drops as your fitness improves, which makes the session feel easier even though the calorie cost changes less than the effort suggests. This is normal, not a problem.

A lot. Sand comes out at roughly 1.8 times the cost of pavement at the same load and pace in the Pandolf model. A 5% hill adds roughly 55% more burn per mile. Adding 10 lbs to the pack on flat ground adds only about 16-19 calories an hour for a 180 lb person, so terrain and hills are often the more practical lever.

Yes, but the accuracy decreases for very short sessions. The equations predict steady-state effort, and the first few minutes of any walk run below that. For 15-20 minute rucks, expect the estimates to be slightly high.

